{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>For I, YHWH your Elohim, am a jealous El.</b> The meaning is not simply the language of jealousy, for a mighty one grows zealous only against one as mighty as himself; rather, 'jealous' is as Rashi explained: He exacts retribution, etc. And this is why He specified the nature of this zeal and said, 'visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children.' And the reason for 'upon the third and upon the fourth [generation],' the commentators said, is that those generations the sinner can see in his lifetime, and his eyes will behold His hand. And it appears to me that this is the reason for 'and doing kindness to thousands [of them that love me]': for the righteous are called alive even in their death, and if so, even in his death he will see his reward and his labor before him when he sees his children prospering with the kindness of an everlasting Elohim. And the reason that the children are held for the iniquity of their fathers: if they are young, they are like a branch of the tree, and a fetus is part of his mother's and father's body. And if they are grown and hold the deeds of their fathers in their hands, they are still attached to their fathers on the side of deeds, and they are like a branch attached to the tree. Therefore it is fitting that they bear double for all their sins, for in their iniquity and the iniquity of their fathers they shall perish. But if they do not hold the deeds of their fathers in their hands, they are like a branch cut off from the tree entirely, and they have no attachment or bond with their fathers." "And as for what He employed here the language of פְּקִידָה and here the language of עֲשִׂיָּה — we already explained above, in the pericope of וַיֵּרָא, on the verse ״וַה׳ פָּקַד אֶת שָׂרָה״ (Genesis 21:1), the distinction between פְּקִידָה and עֲשִׂיָּה: that there is greater actuality in עֲשִׂיָּה than in פְּקִידָה. And always the good measure is multiplied; therefore in the punishment He used the language of פְּקִידָה, which is merely a remembrance in general, but in the good measure He said ״וְעֹשֶׂה חֶסֶד״ — actual doing in practice, for He is an El who works deliverances, and from the mouth of the Most High evil does not go forth in practice. And in the bad measure He said 'upon children' and did not say 'to children' — for 'upon children' means at a time when they hold the deeds of their fathers in their hands, and in addition to the iniquity of the children, the iniquity of their fathers is also remembered against them. But in the good measure He said 'to thousands' and did not say 'upon thousands,' to say that even at a time when the children have no merit of their own, nevertheless they benefit from the merit of their fathers. And this promise is what has stood for us in every generation, that even a generation that is entirely guilty benefits from the merit of its fathers."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:46.189570786Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}